# Untitled

In Situ GIWAXS Analysis of Solvent and Additive Effects on PTB7 Thin Film Microstructure Evolution during Spin Coating
The influence of solvent and processing additives on the pathways and rates of crystalline morphology formation for spin-coated semiconducting PTB7 (poly[[4,8-bis[(2-ethylhexyl)oxy]benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene-2,6-diyl][3-fluoro-2-[(2-ethylhexyl)-carbonyl]-thieno[3,4-b]thiophenediyl]]) thin films is investigated by in situ grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) and optical reflectance, to better understand polymer solar cell (PSC) optimization approaches. In situ characterization of PTB7 film formation from chloroform (CF), chlorobenzene (CB), and 1,2-dichlorobenzene (DCB) solutions, as well as CB solutions with \(1\%\) and \(3\%\) v/v of the processing additives 1-chloronaphthalene (CN), diphenylether (DPE), and 1,8-diiodooctane (DIO), reveals multiple crystallization pathways with: (i) single-solvent systems exhibiting rapid \((<3\ s)\) crystallization after a solvent boiling point-dependent film thinning transition, (ii) solvent \(^+\) additive systems exhibiting different crystallization pathways and crystallite formation times from minutes (CN, DPE) to \(1.5\ h\) (DIO). Identifying crystalline intermediates has implications for bulk-heterojunction PSC morphology optimization via optimized spin-casting processes.
Research in polymer solar cells \((\mathrm{PSCs})^{[1-5]}\) and thin film transistors \((\mathrm{TFTs})^{[6-8]}\) is motivated both by advancing fundamental science and by significant cost reductions that derive from manufacturing by solution processes (e.g., spin coating, blade coating, printing, etc.).[1,2,9,10] Although PSC materials design has advanced in the areas of electron donor/acceptor energetic alignment and interfacial layer function and has enabled recent
power conversion efficiencies (PCE) of \(>13\%\),[11] achieving optimal morphological organization, as indexed by high short circuit current \((J_{\mathrm{sc}})\), fill factor (FF), and PCE, remains a challenge.[12] Numerous studies have established close connections between these device performance metrics and film morphology that are typically optimized by empirical adjustment of variables such as casting solvent, temperature, additives, and concentration, as well as polymer aggregation tendencies.[13–15] Nevertheless, the physicochemical basis of these optimization rules remains obscure and highly materials dependent.
The primary thin film growth method for most PSCs is spin casting from solution, a robust, commonly used laboratory scale method of creating and evaluating thin films for devices.[16] A major challenge in spin-coating PSC active layers has been achieving the material specific optimum
thin film morphology, e.g., crystallinity, domain size, and intercomponent mixing, necessary for optimum performance. To scale processing of these materials toward manufacturing, a thorough understanding of the film growth mechanisms that favor high-performance morphologies in the initial casting is needed. Here, besides the materials themselves, two of the most important fabrication variables are the choice of solvent and/or additive for the spin-coating process.
There are abundant literature examples where seemingly simple alterations in solvent and additive induce major changes in active layer film morphology and PSC performance.[17–30] A classic example is the PTB7/PC71BM bulk heterojunction (BHJ) blend spun from chlorobenzene (CB) solution. Addition of \(3\%\) v/v 1,8-diiodooctane (DIO) increases the PCE from \(<4\%\) to \(>7\%\) (fully optimized devices exceed \(9\%\))[17,18] largely through an additive driven increase in donor/acceptor intercalation in the resulting BHJ film. Note that PTB7 (poly[[4,8-bis[(2-ethylhexyl)oxy]benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene-2,6-diyl][3-fluoro-2-[(2-ethylhexyl)-carbonyl]-thieno[3,4-b]thiophenediyl]]) initiated a seminal jump in PSC efficiency[17] and a donor–acceptor design motif that has been the basis for numerous other high-performance PSCs.[31] Thus, PTB7 represents an ideal model polymer for fundamental studies of film formation dynamics, with a view toward understanding the ground rules governing the film processing for optimum PSC performance. Previous
PTB7 studies in this laboratory focused on the self-folding of the pristine polymer in solution[32] and how DIO impacts PTB7 and \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) aggregation in solution.[33] With these studies as background, we now investigate the influence of solvent and processing additives on PTB7 crystallization pathways and morphology evolution during spin coating. To accomplish this, we employ in situ grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) and optical reflectance. It will be seen that, as a function of solvent and additive, multiple and kinetically distinct PTB7 crystallization phases are operative and that this information has implications for optimizing BHJ PSC morphology and device performance.
Currently, optimal BHJ morphologies are typically discovered by system-specific empirical searching with solvents such as chloroform (CF), CB, and dichlorobenzene (DCB), often combined with additives such as DIO,[19–25] 1-chloronaphthalene (CN),[20,21] and diphenylether (DPE).[26–28] For each additive there are cases where significant morphological changes greatly enhance PSC performance in PTB7-based[17,28–30] and other systems.[19–24,26,27,34] Here GIWAXS has proven to be a powerful tool for characterizing crystallinity, orientation, and donor– acceptor intermixing.[35–39] However, to date, the great majority of such PSC morphological studies were performed ex situ, after film casting and drying.[13–16,40–47] Recently, a number of in situ studies have focused on BHJ film drop casting, blade coating, and slot die casting.[48–50] As well as on steady-state optical analysis of spin coating,[57–59] However, only a few studies have focused on systematic in situ characterization of crystalline morphology during spin coating.[60–64] Consequently, there is limited understanding of how morphology evolution kinetics and pathways during spin coating are affected by solvent, additive, and other factors. Greater understanding of the kinetic pathways for intermediate states leading to the final film morphology should enhance PSC performance and reproducibility. Note that spin coating with in situ GIWAXS analysis presents nontrivial experimental challenges associated with incorporating a spin-coater having extremely high mechanical stability in-line with an X-ray scattering facility. Thus, very few studies have demonstrated the power of in situ scattering for analyzing crystalline morphology during PSC spin coating, leaving this vital part of film formation underexplored. An exception is the important work of Amassian and co-workers who used GIWAXS analysis during spin coating and observed crystalline domain evolution in selected polymer and small-molecule systems.[60–64]
Understanding solvent and additive effects on solution processing is challenging in polymer electronics, especially for BHJ PSC systems, stemming from the multidimensional parameter space of molecular structures, solvent characteristics, temperature, solution concentration, spin speeds, and material ratios. In this communication, as a prelude to tackling more complex BHJ systems, we explore the impact of a subset of these parameters, namely solvents and additives, on pristine PTB7 film formation. Given the recent developments of high-performance nonfullerene acceptors,[65–67] and the possibility that different morphological impacts will be seen in different BHJ systems,[68] we chose to focus on a single model high-performance polymer which has implications for many other similarly designed donor–acceptor polymers as well as BHJ systems.
Here we assess the impacts of solvent and additives on crystalline formation in PTB7 films utilizing the new in situ spincoating GIWAXS system at beamline 8-ID-E of the Argonne National Laboratory Advanced Photon Source, to systematically investigate pristine PTB7 films spun from: (i) a series of common solvents with increasing boiling points: CF (b.p. \(61\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , CB \((\mathrm{b.p.}131\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , and DCB (b.p. \(200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and (ii) a series of common processing additives: CN (b.p. \(263\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , DPE (b.p. \(258\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , and DIO (b.p. \(168\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) all mixed with CB in \(1\%\) and \(3\%\) v/v ratios. These data sets should provide insight into solvent and additive effects because they span the most common variables in polymer film casting while maintaining sufficient simplicity to avoid factors that might obscure mechanistic conclusions. The evolution of the PTB7 lamellar (100) and \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking (010) reflections is analyzed during spin coating with simultaneous optical reflectance data acquisition to monitor film thickness evolution.
Solvent Dependence of Additive-Free PTB7 Film Morphology Evolution: When spin coated from a single additive-free solvent (Figure 1), the PTB7 crystallization pathway is largely independent of the solvent. A sharp dimensional thinning of the deposited solution layer due to solvent evaporation during spin coating initiates rapid formation of ordered face-on lamellae and \(\pi\) -stacking crystalline structures in the film. Figure 1D shows the thickness evolution of films spun from all three solvents. All three solution coatings undergo an abrupt decrease in thickness, indicating the film thinning transition. The transition times \(\left(t_{\mathrm{thin}}\right)\) after the onset of spinning (defined as time \(=\) zero with a precision of \(\pm0.2\mathrm{~s~}\) ) trend with the solvent boiling point, with \(t_{\mathrm{thin}}=2.7\) , 9.0, and \(38.2\mathrm{~s~}\) , corresponding to solutions in CF \((\mathrm{b.p.}\,=\,61\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , CB (b.p. \(=\,131\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , and DCB \((\mathrm{b.p.}=180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , respectively. Figure 1A depicts the film crystalline growth through in-plane \((q_{x y})\) and out-of-plane \((q_{z})\) line cuts of the 2D GIWAXS scattering patterns showing full growth along both the (100) and (010) lattice dimensions. PTB7 films spun from CF go through a rapid crystallization transition from 1.6 to \(3.7\mathrm{~s~}\) , followed by a slower secondary transition which is complete by \(8.1\ s\) without significant changes in the peak positions and line shapes. This two-step evolution possibly results from the initial thinning transition driven growth occurring during the spin-coater acceleration period, with the shift to the secondary transition occurring when the spin-coater finishes accelerating and reaches the \(1000\,\mathrm{\rpm}\) operating speed. For PTB7 films spun from CB, there is an initial scattering feature \((q=1.4\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\) due to the solvent, and as the solution thins, a very rapid transition to the fully crystallized film, beginning at \(6.6\ s\) and complete by \(9.6\ s\) . Finally, PTB7 in DCB \((\mathrm{b.p.}\,=\,180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) undergoes transitions similar to those in CB, with the solvent scattering before thinning occurring at a different \(q\) than in CB due to the different solvent structure, followed by a sharp transition to ordered polymer aggregates starting at \(35.7\ s\) and complete by \(38.1\ s\) . For all three systems, the GIWAXS peak heights, positions, and width (FWHM) (Figure S16, Supporting Information) are static after the initial formation. That is, all three films appear to have their primary, and in the CB and DCB cases, total, crystalline growth occur concurrent with the thinning transition, indicating that the crystallization kinetics are limited to the rapid solvent drying transition, which in turn is governed by boiling point. This result is largely consistent
with those for single solvent small-molecule OPV systems reported elsewhere.[61]
PTB7 Film Morphology Evolution in Solvent/Additive Mixtures: The effects of processing additives on PTB7 film morphology evolution were next examined. Figure 2 shows the crystalline evolution of films spun from polymer solutions with \(3\%\) v/v of additives CN, DPE, and DFO, all in CB (see Supporting Information for data on films spun with \(1\%\) v/v additives). From the optical reflectance data (Figure 2C) note that the sharp drop in film thickness, indicating the thinning transition, is essentially coincident with the film formation from pure CB (Figure 1); however, after the thinning transition, there is a gradual decrease in film thickness as the relatively high boiling additive slowly evaporates. The other panels of Figure 2 show the GIWAXS results, where it is apparent that films spun from solutions having the three additives traverse two distinct crystallite formation pathways and kinetic scenarios, with distinct intermediate structures and formation times.
In films spun with \(3\%\) CN and \(3\%\) DPE, similar morphological evolution is observed. As the primary solvent evaporates in the initial thinning transition, scattering from the additive becomes apparent. For CN, this is evident from the
isotropic ring at \(q=1.68\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) and for DPE, the isotropic ring at \(q=1.4\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\). This additive scattering is clearly visible in the line cuts of Figure 2A (top row and middle row), and in the intermediate 2D scattering pattern (Figure 2B) at \(10~\mathrm{s}\) after initiation of spinning. Wide-angle X-ray scattering from solvents is a well-established phenomenon in solution X-ray scattering experiments[69,70] and the scattering profiles of the pure additive liquids are shown in Figure S14 in the Supporting Information. The additive scattering dominates from 7.7 to \(35.0~\mathrm{s}\) for \(3\%\) CN and 7.3 to \(61\,\mathrm{~s~}\) for \(3\%\) DPE. After that, a transition is apparent as the polymer scattering is first evident and eventually the peak position in the scattering profile shifts from additive scattering to the known \(\pi\) -face-on PTB7 morphology in the late time 2D images of Figure 2B. This scattering shift is apparent in the line cuts as the in-plane \((q_{xy})\) solvent scattering fades and the out-of-plane \((q_{z})\) scattering shifts from the solvent peak to the face-on \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking peak. Additionally, this shift is evident in the plot of \(q_{z}\) peak position in Figure 2D as the peak remains in a constant \(q_{z}\) position defined only by solvent scattering before moving toward the final \(\pi\) -face-on position as crystallization begins. FWHM analysis (Figure S16, Supporting Information) confirms these shifts and the time required to
accomplish them. The growth of (100) domains can be seen, and the shift from additive scattering to polymer (010) growth is readily discerned. Shifts for \(1\%\) CN and \(1\%\) DPE are also evident in Figure 2D, revealing that they go through the same process as the \(3\%\) additive variations, but on a shortened time scale.
Finally, the morphological evolution of the DIO-containing films differs markedly from those with the other two additives, in that no crystallization occurs over the \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) window used for the latter films. Far longer time windows are required for the PTB7–DIO films: \(1\;\mathrm{{h}}\) for \(1\%\ \mathrm{v/v}\) DIO and \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) for \(3\%\) DIO. With \(3\%\) DIO, three distinct phases are detected during PTB7 film growth: (1) the period after the solvent thinning transition
from 8.2 to \(580\;\mathrm{~s~}\) , during which no diffraction features are observed; (2) beginning at \(600~\mathrm{s}\) , an isotropic amorphous ring emerges; and (3) at \(1800~\mathrm{s}\) , transitioning to the \(\pi\) -face-on morphology begins and is essentially complete by \(5580~\mathrm{s}\) . The isotropic ring is assigned to an amorphous aggregate intermediate rather than to additive scattering because the latter would be observed at earlier times. Similar isotropic rings at that q range have been assigned to amorphous polymer scattering in other PSC systems, typically associated with van der Waals interactions between the flexible side chains in amorphous aggregates.[44,71–74] While it is possible that this feature is the result of additive scattering via a more complex mechanism, the time scale of the complete formation would be unchanged. For \(1\%\)
DIO, the intermediate isotropic amorphous scattering ring is not detected. Instead, the film immediately transitions from a long period of no peaks to emergence of the \(\pi\)-face-on form, starting at \(840~\mathrm{s}\) and reaching completion by \(2400~\mathrm{s}\). Crystallization transitions detected in GIWAXS and thickness transitions by optical reflectance are summarized in Figure 3 for all solvent and additive combinations.
Optical absorption spectra of dilute PTB7 solutions containing the various additives are shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information. Note that the additives do not cause changes in the solution phase optical absorption spectra, indicating no significant changes in solution phase PTB7 aggregation.[75,76] There is a slight blue shift and relative decrease of the 0–0 to 0–1 vibronic peak ratio in CF as opposed to other solvents, indicating slightly reduced aggregation since CF is expected have greater solubilizing power.[77]
AFM characterization was performed to assess how the solvent combinations impact final surface morphology, and images are shown in Figure S17 in the Supporting Information. For films spun from single solvents, and from solvents containing DIO and DPE and \(1\%\) CN, the resulting films are smooth and featureless with low rms roughness \((<1\ \mathrm{nm})\), with roughness increasing with increased additive concentration. For films spun from solvents with \(3\%\) CN, the films exhibit broad features with a larger rms roughness of \(5.02\;\mathrm{nm}\).
Organic field effect transistors (OFETs) were fabricated to assess the impact of crystallization dynamics on resulting charge transport properties and are summarized in Table S18 in the Supporting Information with full transfer curves in Figure S18 in the Supporting Information. The OFETs were fabricated on \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) as the gate electrode to ensure an identical spin-coating surface to that used in the GIWAXS experiments. This affords lower mobilities than might be expected for a fully optimized PTB7 device on a higher \(k\) dielectric, but offers a realistic intercomparison of film processing conditions, transport, and diffraction results. Hole mobilities range from 7.08 \((\pm1.99)\times10^{-4}\) (99:1 CB:DIO) to 2.09 \((\pm0.57)\times10^{-3}\) (99:3 CB:DPE) \(\mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) for the different spin-casting conditions.
While these mobilities are within a close range, note that the films processed with DCB and \(1\%\) and \(3\%\) DIO have the lowest OFET mobilities (Table S18, Supporting Information). This is in accord with the reduced X-ray peak intensities observed for these films (Figures 1 and 2) and likely reflects reduced total crystallinity. Note however that the close range of mobilities is also consistent with the GIWAXS and AFM data that the postprocessing, final film characteristics do not differ greatly despite the divergent processing pathways.
The markedly different PTB7 crystalline evolution patterns revealed here by in situ GIWAXS offer new insights into PSC film formation pathways and solvent/additive strategies for optimizing device performance. The similar crystallization dynamics in CF, CB, and DCB (Figure 1) argue that solvent choice can be made largely on the basis of solubility and resulting film quality, as is common practice. In the additive cases, however, more complex mechanisms may be operative depending on the interactions among polymers, solvents, and additives at the molecular level. Thus, on adding CN or DPE to the processing solutions, the PTB7 films evolve post thinning in similar ways, initially entering a period where the primary solvent has largely evaporated, but the additive remains, swelling the film in a period where the scattering is entirely dominated by the additive. It is then followed by a \(1{-}3\,\mathrm{\min}\) transition to \(\pi\)-face-on aggregates, depending on additive concentration. This is evident both in the slow fall in film thickness on this time scale and, more importantly, the slow transition in the GIWAXS from additive dominated to ordered \(\pi\)-face-on aggregate dominated. While slight evolutionary differences between films spun from CN and DPE solutions are evident, the basic mechanism is similar, with slow evaporation of the high-boiling point solvent dictating the morphological kinetics. This mirrors what is observed in spin-coating studies with small molecules,[63] and blade-coating studies[48] with other, but less self-aggregating,[32] polymers.
While the present single solvent \(+\ \mathrm{CN}\) or DPE results correlate well with established models, the film formation process with DIO differs markedly. Although the DIO boiling point is
significantly below that of the other additives, the timescale of final PTB7 film formation is an order of magnitude longer than for the CN- or DPE-containing films, and an additional scattering phase is detected during the film formation. DIO processed films begin in a nondiffracting state, transition to an isotropic scattering pattern—tentatively assigned to an amorphous polymer aggregate—and finally transition to PTB7's typical ordered \(\pi\)-face-on scattering. The long formation time seen here may provide some insight into previous results that showed residual DIO remains in PTB7-Th films before vacuum exposure.[78] However, This result stands in contrast to small-molecule systems where DIO promotes rapid final film formation on the 10–40 s timescale,[61–63] arguing that a very different mechanism operates in this solution-phase self-aggregating polymer system. A plausible explanation is the possibility of a uniquely strong polymer–DIO interaction, allowing the DIO to remain in the film for substantially longer times than the boiling point alone would imply. To investigate this possibility, we consider Hansen solubility parameters and the resulting interaction radii, \(R_{\mathrm{a}}\), were used to approximate polymer–solvent molecule affinities from similarities in dispersion, polar, and hydrogen-bonding components of the solvent–polymer cohesive energy density,[79–82] where lower \(R_{\mathrm{a}}\) indicates greater solvent–polymer affinity. Experimental Hansen solubility parameters for PTB7 have not to our knowledge been determined, so we constructed an approximate PTB7 parameter, using the group contribution method of Richter and co-workers,[83] modified from Van Krevlen and Te Nijenhuis.[84] For additives, the literature Hansen parameters were used[20,82,85,86] and approximate \(R_{\mathrm{a}}\) values then computed for the three additives \(+\) PTB7 (see Section S13 in the Supporting Information for details). This yields \(R_{\mathrm{a}}=4.9\), 3.1, and 7.5 for CN, DPE, and DIO, respectively, indicating that DIO actually has the least affinity for the polymer since a larger solubility space indicates a less favorable interaction. This trend is similar to the b.p. trend and appears inconsistent with the order of magnitude longer film formation time observed for DIO processed films. If, however, the \(\pi\)-electron rich PTB7 model is replaced with a model polymer consisting entirely of saturated PTB7 ethylhexyloxy side chains, we obtain \(R_{\mathrm{a}}=8.8\), 8.0, and 4.2 for CN, DPE, and DIO, respectively. While these results are admittedly qualitative, the stark contrast between the two models provides insight into the differing molecular interactions of these additives with PTB7. Thus, saturated DIO has more favorable interactions with the side chains of the polymer than the \(\pi\)-core, whereas aromatic CN and DPE exhibit the opposite characteristics. That the bulk of these additive differences arise from the dispersion component of the Hansen parameters (Section S13, Supporting Information) is in accord with this picture.
Thus, a plausible scenario for DIO-processed films is that the DIO molecules interact strongly with the PTB7 side chains in the solution phase and then slowly separate over the prolonged drying period. This progression agrees with the appearance of an additional nondiffracting intermediate phase before the amorphous scattering ring appears, followed by the ordered \(\pi\)-face-on diffraction. In this scenario, the significant DIO quantities interacting with the polymer interrupt any periodicity that would be detected by GIWAXS. Once sufficient DIO has exited, a morphology having amorphous scattering would dominate
until the film organized in the final \(\pi\)-face-on morphology. Furthermore, previous work in this laboratory identified a slight expansion of solution-phase PTB7 aggregate dimensions by X-ray scattering, from 34.2 to 36.7 Å on DIO addition,[33] supporting such a DIO interaction model. In principle CN and DPE could also undergo intercalation into PTB7 aggregates, however this seems unlikely. Both additives have similar boiling points and their film morphological evolution rates are similar in films with 1% and 3% additives. It is possible that the self-folding nature of solution-phase PTB7[32] and the saturated side chains reduce CN and DPE molecule access to the conjugated polymer backbone. If these higher boiling additives were strongly bound to the backbone within the aggregates, longer film formation times might be expected as with DIO, and divergence between the two aromatic additives types might be expected from their different molecular architectures and presumably different binding characteristics.
Significant DIO–PTB7 interactions may also relate to additive effects on PTB7: \(\mathbf{PC}_{71}\mathbf{BM}\) PSC performance. It was previously suggested that enhanced performance reflects increased additive-driven interposition between the BHJ donor and acceptor domains, increasing exciton splitting efficiency, hence free charge carrier generation[87] It was also posited that DIO addition facilitates \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) aggregate dissolution and enhances BHJ morphology,[33] however the present results argue that DIO effects on the donor polymer morphology are likely of comparable importance. DIO embedding and long timescale film formation with multiple intermediates may also facilitate fullerene incorporation into swelled PTB7 domains, thereby enhancing \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\).
Note also that the timescales of the present results may help to explain BHJ PSC performance reproducibility issues. Typical active layer spin-coating times are \(\approx45{-}90\;\mathrm{s}\), after which the substrate is removed from the spin-coater, followed by annealing, interfacial layer application, and/or electrode vacuum deposition. However, the film formation times observed here imply a possibility that postspinning device fabrication steps might be carried out before polymer film consolidation/crystallization is complete resulting in a difficult to reproduce, metastable morphology. Thus, care must be exercised in reporting of fabrication results since the time between steps can be important. Additionally, the timescales of these processes must be considered when translating spin coating to other methodologies such as roll-to-roll processing.
In conclusion, we report on the differing film morphology evolution pathways in spin-coating PTB7 films from different solvents (CF, CB, DCB) containing different concentrations of processing additives (CN, DPE, DIO), as probed by in situ GIWAXS and optical reflectance spectroscopy. For pure solvents, crystallite formation is very rapid, occurring within 3 s of the film thinning transition, the onset of which is governed primarily by the solvent boiling point. In contrast, the duration of the transition exhibits little correlation with solvent boiling point, possibly showing a slight inverse relationship. When an additive is present, significantly longer crystallite formation times, from minutes to nearly 1.5 h, are observed. These additive-dependent film formation rates suggest the important role of specific additive–polymer interactions, determined by additive molecular properties and preferential interactions with
different parts of the polymer structure. The present PSC/DIO results differ markedly from additive effects reported in small-molecule OPV systems, indicating that additives may bind strongly within PTB7 aggregates, extending film formation times and possibly explaining DIO effects on PSC morphology and performance.
From a broader perspective, this study shows that in situ GIWAXS combined with a high mechanical stability spin-coating system should enable future studies of diverse optoelectronic film-casting processes (organic, inorganic, and hybrid photovoltaic and transistor materials) to elucidate crystalline formation phase evolution and their kinetics. Such information will open the opportunity to rationally control fabrication conditions for optimized film morphologies targeted to specific applications.
## Experimental Section
Polymer and Solvents: PTB7 was purchased from ONE-Material Inc. and used as received. CF (anhydrous \(\geq99\%\) with \(0.5–1\%\) EtOH as stabilizer), CB (anhydrous \(\geq99.8\%\) ), DCB (anhydrous \(\geq99\%\) ), DPE (selectophore \(\geq99.9\%\) ) and DIO \((98\%\) with \(\mathsf{C u}\) stabilizer) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich, and CN \((\geq97\%)\) from TCI; all were used without further purification. All solvents were stored in an Ar atmosphere glove box. Solutions were mixed in the Ar glove box and stirred overnight at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Spin Casting: Films were spin cast by remotely depositing \(35~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(10\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) polymer solution via syringe pump (New Era Pump Systems NE-1000 at a rate of \(14.6~\upmu\mathrm{L}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}\)) onto bare, cleaned (20 min sonication in methanol, acetone and isopropanol), Si (N-type Antimony Doped \({<}100\,\mathrm{s}\)) substrates and then spinning at \(1000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) (with an acceleration of \(20\ s\ s^{-2}\)) for \(60\ s\) and then allowing them to stand for the remainder of the data acquisition period. The spin coater was custom produced by Aerotech and has an air bearing spindle motor with a vacuum chuck, allowing speeds between 500 and 4000 rpm. The depositions were carried out in a He-purged chamber. A sensor confirmed that the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) level was \({<}1\%\) for all depositions.
In Situ Spin-Coating GIWAXS Data Collection: GIWAXS data were collected at beamline 8-ID-E of the Advanced Photon Source.[88] To provide high-quality data with high temporal and spatial resolution, the X-ray beam was focused to \({<}10\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) vertically at the sample by a Be compound refractive lens and then attenuated by \(50\%\) to reduce sample damage. Three different protocols were used to follow the morphological evolution of the sample over different time windows: \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) (Standard), \(1.0~\mathrm{h}\), and \(2.0~\mathrm{h}\). The two longer acquisition times were only used with the CB:DIO solvent combinations since all other films showed full formation within the \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) window as indicated by negligible changes in scattering pattern in the ensuing time periods. For all three acquisition protocols, GIWAXS images were collected every \(100\;\mathrm{ms}\) over the first \(60\ s\) with each exposure extending for \(96~\mathrm{ms}\), followed by \(4~\mathrm{ms}\) for readout of the pixel array detector (Pilatus 1M, with fast option capable of up to 199 frames \(\mathsf{s}^{-1}\)). After the spinning process, \(96~\mathrm{ms}\) exposures were taken at the following intervals: \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) acquisition: 120 exposures every \(1\ s\), followed by 12 exposures every \(10\ s\), followed by 15 exposures every \(20\ s\); 1 h acquisition: 120 exposures every \(1\ s\), followed by 12 exposures every \(10\ s\), followed by 15 exposures every \(20\ s\), followed by 20 exposures every \(30\ s\), followed by 40 exposures every \(60\ s\); 2 h acquisition: 60 exposures every \(2\ s\), followed by 12 exposures every \(10\ s\), followed by 15 exposures every \(20\ s\), followed by 110 exposures every \(60\ s\). The start of timing is always defined as the point when spinning begins. Full data processing for each film analyzed by in situ GIWAXS is provided in the Supporting Information, while the pertinent information for the differing crystallization kinetics is presented in Figures 1 and 2. A diagram of the experimental setup is shown in the Supporting Information and a full manuscript describing this experimental setup is in preparation.
In Situ Spin-Coating Optical Reflectance Data Collection: Simultaneous with the GIWAXS experiments, visible reflectance data were taken with a Filmetrics F20 instrument at \(200~\mathrm{ms}\) intervals to assess film thickness. All thicknesses were determined using the Filmetrics software package, using a fast Fourier transform (FFT) model during the periods before the thinning transition when the thickness is dominated by solvent, and the refractive index \((n)\) and extinction coefficients \((k)\) were calculated from the reflectance data after the system had transitioned to the thin film form.
UV–vis Absorption Measurements: UV–vis absorption measurements were taken with a Shimadzu UV-3600 spectrometer using quartz cuvettes to contain the PTB7 solutions in all solvent/additive combinations. Data are presented in the Supporting Information.
Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM) Measurements: AFM characterization was performed using a Bruker ICON Atomic Force Microscope in soft tapping mode. All films were spun in an Ar glove box with identical spinning conditions to that used at the beamline. Films were left to dry overnight before characterization. Data are presented in the Supporting Information.
Organic Field Effect Transistors: OFETs were fabricated on top of p-doped silicon coated with \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) of thermally grown oxide (WRS Materials) as the dielectric layer that doubled as the gate electrode. \(\mathsf{S i O}_{2}\) substrates were cleaned by sonication for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\) in acetone/hexanes/ ethanol and then illuminated under UV/zone for 5 min. All films were spun in an Ar glove box with identical spinning conditions to that used at the beamline. Gold source and drain electrodes \((40\ \mathsf{n m})\) were deposited by thermal evaporation through a shadow mask to give a channel with \(L=50\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) and \(W=1000\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\). Devices were tested in the dark in ambient using an Agilent B1500A semiconductor parameter analyzer. Mobilities were calculated in the saturation regime and the capacitance was taken to be \(11\ \mathsf{n F\ c m}^{-2}\).
